@@ -5,5 +5,6 @@                                                                                                
 This document collects the scientific literature regarding **brain**.                                         
                                                                                                               
 ### Brain Imaging, Segmentation, and Clinical Applications                                                    
 Recent developments in neuroimaging and medical image analysis have significantly improved the precision of   
brain tumor segmentation and disease progression modeling. Deep learning frameworks like **NeuroTS-Net** and   
**De-GAN** have advanced multi-class semantic segmentation of pediatric brain tumors and contrast-enhancing    
FLAIR synthesis, respectively, outperforming baseline methods in Dice scores and handling domain shifts        
[^2609.16873][^2609.16755]. Foundation models such as **BrainVLM** integrate multi-modal data to classify WHO  
brain tumor types with diagnostic uncertainty quantification [^2609.16597], while **GRIN+** addresses machine  
unlearning for imbalanced medical data to protect patient privacy without compromising rare condition diagnosis
[^2609.15571]. Furthermore, **nnU-Net** generalization across heterogeneous brain tumor populations has been   
rigorously evaluated, highlighting challenges in small tumor volumes [^2609.15524]. In the realm of diffusion  
MRI, **DTI-SHNet** enables single-to-multi-shell synthesis, preserving downstream diffusion measures           
[^2609.14312]. Clinical assessment tools have also evolved, with **TractSpLearn** detecting subtle white matter
alterations in traumatic brain injury [^2609.15342] and reverse spatio-temporal modeling reconstructing        
healthier brain states from Alzheimer's disease progression [^2609.14590]. Ex-vivo calf brain models with      
integrated force sensors now allow high-fidelity quantification of tool-tissue interactions during simulated   
neurosurgery [^2609.14198], and 4D cerebral microvascular imaging platforms visualize hemodynamic responses in 
stroke models [^2609.16666].                                                                                   
                                                                                                               
+Furthermore, accelerated MRI sequences have been enhanced through implicit neural representation              
reconstruction, enabling high-quality 3D MPnRAGE scans in clinically feasible times [^2609.18589].             
Physics-informed deep learning methods now allow for robust denoising and quantitative parameter map           
reconstruction in low-field MRI, generalizing across different field strengths and anatomies without retraining
[^2609.12966]. In the domain of anomaly detection, frameworks like Spatial-FAD combine vision foundation model 
priors with CLIP semantics to improve lesion localization in brain MRI, significantly boosting segmentation    
performance in few-shot scenarios [^2609.12454]. Longitudinal brain aging modeling has also advanced with      
Brain-PACE, a Siamese MRI framework that directly estimates the pace of structural brain aging, showing strong 
associations with cognitive impairment and tau burden [^2609.11378]. On the computational side, deep           
registration pipelines now align complex brain volumes in a single forward pass, drastically reducing          
computation time while preserving high spatial resolution [^2609.11240][^2609.10789]. Additionally, adversarial
robustness in medical hyperspectral imaging has been probed using brain datasets, revealing critical           
vulnerabilities in deep learning models that rely on spectral-spatial dependencies                             
[^10.1007_s44267-026-00129-x].                                                                                 
+                                                                                                              
 ### EEG, Brain Signal Analysis, and Digital Twins                                                             
 The analysis of electroencephalography (EEG) and brain signals has seen a surge in foundation models and      
zero-shot learning capabilities. **METIS** establishes a new paradigm for multi-task brain signal analysis,    
pretrained on over 70,000 hours of recordings and outperforming generalist models in zero-shot evaluations     
[^2609.15740]. Similarly, **iMINDBench** provides a standardized benchmark for iEEG neural decoding across     
institutions, revealing the limits of scaling supervised data [^2609.18104]. Generative decoding has also      
advanced, with **Mind2Cloud** utilizing two-granularity diffusion decoding to reconstruct 3D point clouds from 
EEG signals [^2609.13991]. Transformer-based frameworks with spectrogram representations have improved         
schizophrenia detection from EEG [^2609.14015], while studies on infant EEG microstates link temperament       
attributes to specific brain topographies [^10.7273_000007929]. On the modeling front, **brain-based digital   
twins** (BB-DT) fuse EEG with kinematic data for real-time motor imagery feedback [^10.17870_bathspa.00017805],
and stochastic models combining Random Neural Networks with Markov-Modulated Poisson Processes aim to improve  
the fidelity of brain digital twins [^2609.13973].                                                             
                                                                                                               
+Advancements in EEG and neural signal processing continue to push the boundaries of zero-shot learning and    
multimodal integration. The MAPA framework leverages masked autoencoders with anatomical and positional        
encodings to learn transferable neural representations from iEEG recordings, drastically reducing the labeled  
data required for cross-subject decoding [^2609.13507]. Similarly, FRIST utilizes fMRI-derived class geometry  
to guide the residual refinement of EEG predictions, improving individual-finger BCI decoding without requiring
paired trials at inference [^2609.12298]. Generative and classification models have also been optimized, with  
Random Forest algorithms proving effective for classifying real and imagery motor activities from              
consumer-grade EEG [^2609.11695], and CoMA-DiT employing bidirectional cross-modal diffusion transformers to   
augment multimodal brain state decoding [^2609.11341]. However, pipeline design remains complex, as            
demonstrated by findings that component anti-synergy can occur in P300 spellers, where adding all available    
filters and priors does not universally improve performance [^2609.10961]. Spectral differential network       
analysis methods have further been developed to infer high-dimensional time series changes in EEG data,        
providing robust window size selection and asymptotic normality for de-biased estimators [^2609.13609].        
                                                                                                               
 ### Computational Neuroscience and Brain Connectivity                                                         
 Theoretical and computational approaches continue to unravel brain dynamics and structural fingerprints.      
**Graphlets** serve as structural fingerprints for complex networks, capturing local-to-mesoscale topology in  
resting-state functional connectomes and schizophrenia-related alterations [^2609.17445]. Nonparametric        
correlation estimators solving cubic equations have been applied to analyze dynamic functional connectivity    
across six frequency bands [^2609.13848]. Theoretical models suggest that moderate neural noise, modeled as    
temporally correlated fluctuations, is critical for faithful internal simulation of rare events in Bayesian    
Confidence Propagation Neural Networks (BCPNN) [^2609.18033]. Evolutionary neuro-computational studies         
challenge the Cognitive Buffer Hypothesis, showing that larger neural networks in dynamic environments often   
arise from prior static evolution [^2609.15569]. Additionally, spatial codes governing olfactory receptor      
choice align sensory maps in the nose and brain, revealing a continuously varying transcriptional code for     
smell [^10.5281_zenodo.18941307][^10.5281_zenodo.18941308], while cholecystokinin signaling in the nucleus of  
the solitary tract modulates satiety and is blunted by high-fat diets [^10.7273_000008411].                    
                                                                                                               
+Theoretical and computational neuroscience continues to explore brain dynamics, connectivity, and the         
intersection of biological networks with artificial intelligence. Foundation models for brain recordings are   
now being evaluated for perturbational validity, ensuring they preserve conditional distributions of future    
trajectories under controlled inputs [^2609.12710]. Bayesian hidden Markov random fields have been introduced  
to partition cortical influence into collapsed states, enabling highly detailed local influence maps of        
fingertip tapping responses [^2609.13319]. The spatiotemporal organization of neuronal avalanches is now       
systematically analyzed using pyAvalanches, which computes transition matrices to map activity propagation     
across EEG recordings [^2609.11530]. For MEG data, a regression-based framework utilizing radial basis         
functions and gradient boosting provides spatially resolved cortical activation estimates, overcoming the      
limitations of traditional cluster-based permutation tests [^2609.11278]. The intersection of neuroscience and 
AI is further highlighted by the Platonic brain bridge hypothesis, which posits that human brain networks can  
serve as architectural priors for multimodal large language models [^2609.10947]. Cortical information transfer
studies using permutation transfer entropy reveal that hemispherical network dynamics are conserved across     
human handedness during motor execution [^2609.10870]. Finally, dual-contrastive learning frameworks like      
STAMGC are being applied to spatial transcriptomics to identify finer brain structures and reduce noise in     
domain identification [^10.1101_gr.281999.126].                                                                
+                                                                                                              
 ### Neuro-Oncology, Disease Mechanisms, and Metabolism                                                        
 Research into brain diseases and metabolic profiling continues to expand. Glioblastoma (GBM) remains a focal  
point, with studies on nucleosome repositioning in cfDNA offering non-invasive liquid biopsy diagnostics       
[^10.5526_err-00042862][^10.5526_err-00042900]. Lipid-based nanoparticles and LNP-encapsulated siRNA targeting 
TERT show promise in overcoming the blood-brain barrier for GBM therapy                                        
[^10.17030_uclan.thesis.00059253][^10.17030_uclan.thesis.00059033], while secretomes from neural crest-derived 
stem cells inhibit GBM proliferation and modulate immune responses [^10.48683_1926.00128572]. Pan-cancer brain 
metastasis atlases provide spatially resolved single-cell data                                                 
[^10.5281_zenodo.21816284][^10.5281_zenodo.21816285][^10.5281_zenodo.21782885][^10.5281_zenodo.21782884], and  
RET signaling is identified as a mediator in estrogen receptor-positive breast cancer brain metastasis         
[^10.5281_zenodo.19501083][^10.5281_zenodo.19501082]. Beyond oncology, comprehensive arteriovenous metabolic   
profiling reveals cross-organ metabolite exchange, highlighting brain-specific uptake and release patterns     
[^10.5281_zenodo.20652506][^10.5281_zenodo.20652505]. In neurodegenerative contexts, the amyloid precursor     
protein (APP) is re-evaluated as a universal survival molecule, critiquing broad-spectrum APP suppression      
therapies for Alzheimer's [^10.5281_zenodo.20049820], and nitric oxide signaling is linked to aerobic          
exercise-related neuroprotection in Parkinson's disease via mitophagy modulation                               
[^10.4103_mgr.medgasres-d-26-00008]. Finally, circular RNAs (circRNAs) accumulate in the brain corresponding to
biological age, serving as potential biomarkers for aging and insulin-related pathways [^10.48617_etd.1568],   
while category fluency tasks reveal cultural and age-related gray matter volume associations in semantic       
processing [^10.48617_etd.1671].                                                                               
+                                                                                                              
+Neuro-oncology and disease mechanism research has seen significant strides in metastasis detection,           
therapeutic delivery, and pathological classification. Deep learning ensembles based on nnU-Net have been      
optimized for pre- and post-treatment brain metastases segmentation, with rigorous out-of-fold audits          
confirming the robustness of post-processing cascades [^2609.11477]. Scale-aware 3D deep learning frameworks   
combining different spatial fields of view have improved the precision-false-positive trade-off in brain       
metastasis detection [^2609.10825]. Stereotactic radiosurgery for thalamic metastases demonstrates high local  
control rates, supporting its role as a safe and effective treatment option                                    
[^10.1227_neuprac.0000000000000284], while frailty indices show limited incremental value in predicting        
outcomes for vestibular schwannoma surgeries compared to tumor characteristics                                 
[^10.1227_neuprac.0000000000000289]. Cellular atlases of breast cancer brain metastases reveal prognostically  
distinct immune landscapes, highlighting the need for tailored immunotherapies                                 
[^10.5281_zenodo.17723783][^10.5281_zenodo.17723784]. Therapeutic delivery mechanisms are advancing with       
medical microbubbles, which show potential for bridging the blood-brain barrier in gene therapy and cancer     
treatment [^10.5281_zenodo.19676283][^10.5281_zenodo.19676282]. In neurodegeneration and injury, differential  
DNA methylation in blood is linked to cerebrospinal fluid biomarkers of Alzheimer's disease                    
[^10.1038_s41380-026-03662-9], and artemisinin has been shown to alleviate hippocampal neuronal apoptosis and  
cognitive dysfunction following cardiac arrest [^10.36721_pjps.2026.39.12.334.1]. Qualitative studies also     
explore the potential of psychedelics in traumatic brain injury recovery, noting reductions in trauma-related  
distress and perceived cognitive improvements [^10.3389_fneur.2026.1880026]. Finally, delayed diagnosis remains
a critical challenge in central nervous system intravascular large B-cell lymphoma, where brain biopsy is often
required after prolonged neurological deterioration [^10.52662_nf.2026.00276].                                 
                                                                                                               

